Rewritten: the three MCP servers the plugin ships with no configuration at all
-- servers/hackernews (five tools), servers/arxiv (three) and
servers/npm-registry (three) -- as one Nodecode cell. Each server is a stdio
MCP process over an HTTP client in TypeScript; here the clients are Lisp
functions against the same endpoints (the Hacker News Firebase API, the
Algolia HN search index, export.arxiv.org's Atom query API and
registry.npmjs.org), and the same eleven tools are registered with the model
under their source's prefix, so three namespaces never collide in one tool
list. The rendering follows each server's renderText: a numbered line a
story, paper or package, with the fields the original's schemas carried.

Left out: the `umt` CLI and the two commands that drive it (commands/umt.md,
commands/umt-doctor.md, skills/umt-mcp/SKILL.md) -- listing a registry of 28
MCP servers, merging them into another harness's config files, scaffolding
Agent Skills and self-updating the CLI. None of that is a tool a Nodecode cell
needs: Nodecode talks to MCP servers itself through its own mcp cell, and a
cell that wrote into other applications' config files would be doing to the
operator's machine what Nodecode already does for its own section. The Atom
feed's non-entry resources (the arxiv `feed` resource and the
`literature-review` prompt) are not carried either: a cell's verbs and tools
are its surface, and both were reachable through the three list and search
tools. Caching, the plugin's rate limiter and its config-file backups have no
counterpart here: nothing is written, so nothing needs backing up.
